# Agent comparison root — {{BENCHMARK}} ({{N_TASKS}} tasks, {{MODE}})

Self-contained working dirs (one per task) plus a driver, for running coding agents
on JML-spec synthesis with the same tools, budget and integrity rule as vACT.
Each task dir holds the problem (`Solution.java`) and the tools as shell scripts
(`verify.sh`, `run_specharness.sh` [with-harness arm only], `submit.sh`). The agent
edits `Solution.java`, iterates verify → fix (→ score), then submits.

## Layout

```
.
├── run_agents.py      # one fresh agent session per task dir (lock, retry policy, logs)
├── audit.py           # flag forbidden agent actions -> audit.json
├── usage_report.py    # tokens / time / tool calls -> usage_per_task.csv, usage_summary.json
├── aggregate.py       # pass@{0.25,0.5,0.75,1.0} + mean metrics -> aggregate.json
├── README.md
├── .venv/             # ONE shared venv (javalang) used by every task session
└── <TASK_ID>/         # one self-contained dir per task
    ├── Solution.java  # agent edits this (add JML; class stays `Solution`)
    ├── AGENTS.md      # the prompt/instructions for the agent
    ├── verify.sh  [run_specharness.sh]  submit.sh
    └── harness/       # vendored CLI + tools, task.json, config.json, logs
```

There is no `Test.java` and no `tests/`: the harness tool reads `harness/task.json`,
which holds the first 5 feedback pairs and the feedback invalid inputs (with-harness
arm) or no pairs and no invalid inputs at all (no-harness arm).

## Run

```bash
# one agent over all tasks (built-in templates: claude, codex)
python run_agents.py --agent claude --threads 4 --only-missing

# or a custom agent command (tokens: {agents_md} {agents_md_path} {task_dir} {task_id})
python run_agents.py --cmd 'mytool run --prompt-file {agents_md_path}' --timeout 1200 --only-missing

python audit.py          # -> audit.json
python usage_report.py   # -> usage_per_task.csv / usage_summary.json
```

`--only-missing` re-runs a task only if it never ran or its last session failed for
an infrastructure reason (non-zero exit, timeout, interrupted); every re-run starts
from a fresh state (the previous one is archived in `harness/attempt_<n>/`). A task
whose `harness/run.lock` is held by a live session is skipped.

## Final submission

For every agent the final submission is the code of the **last accepted**
`verify` / `run_specharness` call (`harness/last_valid_Solution.java`; its sha256 is
the `code_sha256` of the last `"accepted": true` line of `harness/tool_calls.jsonl`),
or the original program if no call was accepted. `submit.sh` records it in
`submission.json` and writes it back to `Solution.java`.

## Dependencies

Only the `javalang` pip package and the `openjml` binary are needed. Scaffolding
creates **one shared `.venv`** here at the out-root (with `javalang`); every task's
`*.sh` scripts load it. Resolution order in `_env.sh`: `$VACT_PYTHON` →
`<out-root>/.venv` → a `python` that already has `javalang` → (last resort) build the
shared `.venv`. `openjml` is taken from `$OPENJML` or `PATH`. OpenJML's JVM heap is
capped via `OPENJML_JVM` (default `-Xmx8g`).

## Attempt budget

Each task allows a fixed number of tool attempts (`verify` + `run_specharness`
calls, including calls rejected as `code_modified`), set at scaffold time and stored
in `harness/config.json` — the only source (no environment or command-line
override). When exhausted, the tools refuse and instruct the agent to submit.
`submission.json` records `attempts` used.
